[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] During the early June long weekend overlapping with Memorial Day, Jeonju Hanok Village in Jeonbuk was packed with tourists from around the country and the world. Couples in matching hanbok, foreigners carrying large bags, and families with children strolled along Taejo-ro and Eunhaeng-ro, enjoying the holiday atmosphere.

Some sidewalks were so crowded with tourists lining up for Jeonju&rsquo;s food that there was no room to walk. Inside the Craft Exhibition Hall and souvenir shops, visitors cooled off in the air-conditioning while browsing exhibitions and shopping for mementos. Children and foreign tourists played traditional games like &ldquo;biseok-chigi&rdquo; and &ldquo;jegichagi&rdquo; at experience centers, creating a &ldquo;Korean-style day,&rdquo; and many paused to enjoy the sounds of performances at Daesaseupcheong Square. A 50-meter-long line formed to see the royal portrait at Gyeonggijeon Shrine, while the &ldquo;Jeonju Cultural Heritage Night Tour&rdquo; added further vitality to the village.

With hanok houses, traditional performances, and craft experiences all in one place, this site is more than just a tourist destination&mdash;it&rsquo;s becoming a testing ground for &ldquo;localized K-culture.&rdquo; Yet merchants and visitors alike expressed discomfort and disappointment.


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Tourists participating in the Jeonju Cultural Heritage Night Tour at Gyeonggijeon Shrine. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 202" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3280956_3347897_3441.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Tourists participating in the Jeonju Cultural Heritage Night Tour at Gyeonggijeon Shrine. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 202



◆&ldquo;Unique but lacking&rdquo; &ndash; Merchants&rsquo; Perspective

Jang Hee-sook, head of Chakhan Gongjakso Cooperative and representative of Onsaemiro Workshop, has been working with craft artists in Jeonju for over a decade. She has created souvenirs inspired by local landmarks like Pungnammun Gate, Jeondong Cathedral, and cherry blossoms in Wansan Park. &ldquo;We produce goods based on three elements: Jeonju-inspired design, practicality, and portability,&rdquo; she said, adding that tourists often comment they&rsquo;re &ldquo;unique&rdquo; and &ldquo;pretty.&rdquo;

However, Jang noted that cultural support from the city and province is not clearly felt. &ldquo;While Jeonju is famous for food and that&rsquo;s helped food-related tourism grow, overall consumer spending is shrinking,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Even though festivals are more active lately, cultural and arts-related events and invitations have decreased compared to the past.&rdquo; She continued, &ldquo;We formed a cooperative to promote our work together, focusing on crafts. We&#39;re increasing craft-based experiences. Sustainable, collaborative promotion is more important than one-off publicity.&rdquo;

At the Jeonju Traditional Liquor Museum, tourists interested in traditional Korean alcohol explored the exhibits and sampled makgeolli and other liquors. A museum representative said, &ldquo;Visitors aren&rsquo;t used to traditional liquors, so they find them novel and show a lot of interest. Igangju is especially popular as a Jeonju specialty, and sales and tastings are well received.&rdquo;

However, programs are only available on Saturdays, leaving tourists who visit on other days disappointed. Despite the high foot traffic, trash bins are scarce, leading some to leave garbage near restrooms, which has become a visual nuisance.


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Eunhaeng-ro, Jeonju Hanok Village. Walking through hanok scenery is pleasant, but visitors point to a lack of attractions and food options. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 2025" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3280956_3347901_360.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Eunhaeng-ro, Jeonju Hanok Village. Walking through hanok scenery is pleasant, but visitors point to a lack of attractions and food options. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 2025



Kim Eun-jung (42, female), who has run a hanbok rental shop in Hanok Village for 10 years, stressed the need for new events and improved convenience. &ldquo;Even though the number of visitors to Hanok Village has increased, hanbok rentals have declined,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;Just like hanbok-wearing visitors get free admission at Gyeongbokgung Palace, we need events in Jeonju that promote and celebrate hanbok.&rdquo;

She added, &ldquo;Hanok Village is best enjoyed at a leisurely pace. The roads and sidewalks need improvement, and extending streetlight hours would allow visitors to better enjoy the ambiance.&rdquo; She continued, &ldquo;I often hear people say there&rsquo;s nothing to do here. Even though I live in Hanok Village, there are still many unknown attractions, and many tourists say there&rsquo;s not much to see even in the museums and galleries.&rdquo;

◆Tourists Say: &ldquo;Charming, But Disappointing&rdquo;

Kim Soo-hoon (35, male) visited Jeonju with his girlfriend, drawn by its reputation for food and hanok architecture. They enjoyed dining in Imsil and Jeonju&rsquo;s newer districts during their two-day trip. But about Hanok Village, he said, &ldquo;It didn&rsquo;t feel much different from Insadong in Seoul. Despite its fame, the lack of parking was a real inconvenience.&rdquo;

Kim Mi-ji (29, female) and Park Soo-bin (30, female), who returned to Jeonju after 18 months, said, &ldquo;There are definitely more foreign tourists than before. The information center now has photo zones and lockers, and you can see new buildings going up&mdash;the village seems to be growing.&rdquo;


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Traditional game tools like jegi placed at the “Our Playground Marudal” experience zone. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 2025" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3280956_3347902_3641.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Traditional game tools like jegi placed at the &ldquo;Our Playground Marudal&rdquo; experience zone. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 2025



Still, they couldn&rsquo;t hide their disappointment. &ldquo;The hanok houses and Korean-style souvenirs are interesting for foreigners, but since Jeonju is famous for food, we expected more dining options. Most restaurants were focused on traditional cuisine, which felt limiting,&rdquo; they said. &ldquo;For foreign tourists, it may be a unique experience, but for domestic visitors, the lack of variety is disappointing.&rdquo;

They also pointed out, &ldquo;We had high expectations for Jeonju&rsquo;s food, but with summer heat starting, there are no proper shaded rest areas outside of Gyeonggijeon and Taejo-ro. Trash and bins near the few rest spots also made eating there feel unhygienic.&rdquo;

They added, &ldquo;It seems like only people who already know Jeonju are visiting. Although lockers are installed at the information center, we didn&rsquo;t see anyone use them during our two-day visit. It seems like poor promotion is the biggest issue.&rdquo;

Seo Moon-tak (alias, female) and Moon Ju-chan (alias, male), who visited Jeonju Hanok Village after a stop in Gunsan from Incheon, said, &ldquo;The first impression of the hanok architecture was great, and the food was delicious.&rdquo; But they added, &ldquo;There are a lot of foreign tourists, but beyond the hanok houses, it&rsquo;s hard to feel any deep Korean cultural atmosphere. It&rsquo;s a nice place for couples, but a little underwhelming for groups of friends.&rdquo;

◆399.2 Billion KRW Investment&hellip; The Challenge: A Sustainable Ecosystem

Jeonbuk Province announced this year its vision to become a hub for &ldquo;K-Culture, Sports, and Tourism Industries&rdquo; under the name Jeonbuk Special Self-Governing Province, and plans to invest 399.2 billion KRW to make these sectors central to regional growth.

To achieve this, the province laid out 19 detailed action plans in four major areas, aiming to enhance residents&rsquo; quality of life by expanding cultural, sports, and tourism infrastructure. It also plans to conduct professional research for the implementation of special administrative projects and discover new national-level initiatives to lay a foundation for future growth.


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Streets of Jeonju Hanok Village. Although the first impression is positive, complaints remain about the limited variety of food and activities. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 2025" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3280956_3347924_1341.jpg" />
[Cheonji Ilbo Jeonbuk=Reporter Kim Dong-hyun] Streets of Jeonju Hanok Village. Although the first impression is positive, complaints remain about the limited variety of food and activities. ⓒCheonji Ilbo, June 12, 2025



Meanwhile, a 2022 policy report by the Korea Culture and Tourism Institute titled &ldquo;Research on Strategies to Expand the Base of the Traditional Cultural Industry&rdquo; stated that although hanbok, hansik (Korean food), and hanji (traditional paper) are representative cultural assets, they still remain primarily experience-based services focused on foreign consumers. The report highlighted a lack of regional brand identity, integration with modern design, and the development of integrated stay-type content.

It further pointed out that traditional cultural content is mostly centered on heritage experiences or local government-led festivals and has not grown into a private sector-driven content industry. In order to enhance the sustainability of traditional culture, the report proposed moving beyond short-term public support to long-term, region-based branding strategies, creating combined experience-tourism products linking hanbok, hansik, and hanji, modernizing menus using local ingredients and reflecting regional identity in branding, strengthening stay-type content to increase repeat visitation, and building collaborative ecosystems involving local residents and private companies.Although tourism based on traditional culture remains popular, especially around Jeonju Hanok Village, the sustainability of experience content and private-sector involvement remains an unresolved issue. For Jeonbuk&rsquo;s strategy to be effective, it must go beyond top-down administration to develop experiences that meet tourist expectations and create partnerships locals can genuinely feel. The province&rsquo;s next challenge lies in how it designs this sustainable ecosystem

